  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can PDF metadata really prove a document was fabricated in court?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes, and it has. Metadata such as file creation dates, modification history, and software signatures left behind by tools like Excel's print-to-PDF function can show that a document was made far more recently than claimed, or made with a different process than the party testified to. Courts have treated this kind of forensic metadata evidence as sufficient grounds for sanctions up to and including dismissal."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What happened in Gilimex v. Amazon?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "A Vietnamese manufacturer sued Amazon for $840 million after their supply relationship ended, then supported part of its damages claim with purchase orders it said were scanned paper originals. A forensic expert found the files had been generated by printing Excel spreadsheets to PDF two days before they were handed over in discovery, not scanned from paper years earlier as claimed. The Southern District of New York dismissed the entire case with prejudice as a sanction for fraud on the court."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Is scrubbing metadata before litigation illegal?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "It depends heavily on timing and process. Once litigation is reasonably anticipated, parties generally have a duty to preserve electronically stored information, including metadata, and unilaterally stripping it before producing documents can draw scrutiny even without bad intent. Williams v. Sprint/United Management Co. established that documents ordered produced in their ordinary business format should generally include metadata unless the parties agree otherwise or the court rules on an objection first."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can metadata alone get a case dismissed?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Metadata is rarely the only factor, but it is frequently the thread that unravels everything else. In Gilimex v. Amazon, the file-creation metadata combined with inconsistencies in physical document seals and a implausible cover story to convince the court that the fabrication went beyond a single bad document, which is what justified dismissing the entire case rather than excluding individual exhibits."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Was the Iraq Dossier case an actual lawsuit?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. The 2003 UK government dossier on Iraq was never the subject of a lawsuit. It's included here because it remains the most widely known example of document metadata changing a real-world outcome, in this case a major political and journalistic one rather than a courtroom verdict."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How do forensic examiners detect a print-to-PDF fabrication?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Examiners typically compare the claimed origin of a document against what its metadata actually shows. A document claimed to be a scan of a years-old paper original should generally not carry a Producer or CreatorTool field pointing to spreadsheet export software, and its creation date should not cluster suspiciously close to the discovery deadline. Examiners also compare metadata across a batch of supposedly unrelated documents, since genuine scans rarely share identical creation timestamps or software fingerprints."}
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "HowTo",
    "name": "What forensic examiners check when a document's authenticity is disputed",
    "totalTime": "PT4M",
    "step": [
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 1, "name": "Compare creation date to modification date", "text": "A document claimed to be years old with a creation date from days before it was produced in discovery is an immediate red flag."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 2, "name": "Check the Producer and CreatorTool fields", "text": "A file claimed to be a scanned paper original should not carry metadata pointing to spreadsheet or word processor export tools."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 3, "name": "Compare metadata across the full batch of documents", "text": "Genuine documents from different dates and sources rarely share identical creation signatures; clustering is a sign of batch fabrication."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 4, "name": "Request the native source files, not just the PDFs", "text": "The underlying spreadsheet or word processor file, with its own independent metadata trail, is often what a party fabricating evidence is most reluctant to produce."}
    ]
  }
  </script>

