    <h2 id="section-4">#4. The Precedent That Started It All</h2>
    <p>Long before "metadata" was a word litigators used casually, a 2005 Kansas federal court case set the terms for how it would be treated in discovery. Shirley Williams and a class of other former employees sued Sprint/United Management Company over an age-discrimination claim tied to a large layoff. The court ordered Sprint to produce the Excel spreadsheets it had used to plan the layoff, in the format Sprint used them in the ordinary course of business. Sprint complied, but only after scrubbing the metadata and locking several of the spreadsheet cells first.</p>
    <p>The presiding magistrate judge found that Sprint's unilateral decision to strip the metadata, without first raising an objection or seeking a protective order, was not acceptable under emerging e-discovery standards. No sanctions were ultimately imposed, because at the time the law around metadata production was still unsettled and the court's own earlier orders had been ambiguous enough to create a reasonable excuse. But the underlying principle, that metadata is part of a document when it's produced in its native business format and not an optional extra a party can quietly remove, became one of the most cited rulings in the field, and shaped how courts have approached the question ever since.</p>

    <h2 id="section-5">#5. Why Metadata Keeps Deciding These Cases</h2>
    <p>There's a reason this pattern repeats across two decades of wildly different disputes. Metadata is generated automatically, by software, without the document's author thinking about it. That's what makes it genuinely difficult to fabricate consistently. A person forging the visible content of a document can control every word on the page. Getting a file's creation timestamp, modification history, and software signature to all agree with a false story is a much harder problem, because those fields are often written by different parts of the software stack, sometimes without the user ever seeing them.</p>
    <p>This is also why forensic examiners don't look at a single field in isolation. A plausible story falls apart when several independent metadata fields all point the same inconvenient direction at once: a creation date that lands suspiciously close to a discovery deadline, a Producer field naming spreadsheet export software on a file claimed to be a scan, a cluster of documents from supposedly unrelated years that all share the same software fingerprint.</p>

    <div class="step-list">
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Compare creation date to modification date</h4>
          <p>A document claimed to be years old with a creation date from days before it was produced is an immediate flag.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Check the Producer and CreatorTool fields</h4>
          <p>A file claimed to be a scanned paper original should not carry metadata pointing to spreadsheet or word processor export tools.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Compare metadata across the full batch of documents</h4>
          <p>Genuine documents from different dates and sources rarely share identical creation signatures. Clustering suggests batch fabrication.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Request the native source files, not just the PDFs</h4>
          <p>The underlying spreadsheet or document, with its own independent metadata trail, is often what a party is most reluctant to produce.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout amber">
      <div class="callout-label">Worth knowing</div>
      <p>There's an important distinction between the metadata hygiene most people practice before sharing a routine document and what's expected once litigation is reasonably anticipated. In the second situation, stripping metadata from files you're obligated to preserve, even for ordinary privacy reasons, can itself become the issue a court examines, as Williams v. Sprint illustrates. The time to clean a document is before it becomes evidence, not after.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Checking a file's own properties is a useful first step before any formal forensic review. It won't settle a disputed case on its own, but it's often what tells you whether something is worth escalating to an expert in the first place.</p>

