    <h2 id="section-6">#6. What This Means Outside the Courtroom</h2>
    <p>Most people reading this will never be party to an $840 million lawsuit, but the underlying habit is the same one that matters for anyone who creates, signs, or exchanges documents professionally: a file's metadata tells a story independent of what's written on the page, and that story is available to anyone who knows to look for it. Lawyers preparing exhibits, journalists vetting leaked documents, and compliance teams reviewing internal records are all, in a smaller way, doing the same check a forensic examiner does in a case like Gilimex, just earlier and usually with lower stakes.</p>

    <div class="callout green">
      <div class="callout-label">Best practice</div>
      <p>If a document is already evidence, or might become evidence, preserve it exactly as it exists: native file, full metadata, no cleanup, and let any sanitization happen through a documented, agreed process rather than unilaterally. The instinct to tidy up a file before sending it is reasonable in day-to-day business, but it is precisely the wrong instinct once litigation is on the table.</p>
    </div>

