    <div class="faq-section">
      <h2 class="faq-section-title">#7. Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Can PDF metadata really prove a document was fabricated in court?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Yes, and it has. Metadata such as file creation dates, modification history, and software signatures left behind by tools like Excel's print-to-PDF function can show that a document was made far more recently than claimed, or made with a different process than the party testified to. Courts have treated this kind of forensic metadata evidence as sufficient grounds for sanctions up to and including dismissal.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          What happened in Gilimex v. Amazon?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">A Vietnamese manufacturer sued Amazon for $840 million after their supply relationship ended, then supported part of its damages claim with purchase orders it said were scanned paper originals. A forensic expert found the files had been generated by printing Excel spreadsheets to PDF two days before they were handed over in discovery, not scanned from paper years earlier as claimed. The Southern District of New York dismissed the entire case with prejudice as a sanction for fraud on the court.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Is scrubbing metadata before litigation illegal?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">It depends heavily on timing and process. Once litigation is reasonably anticipated, parties generally have a duty to preserve electronically stored information, including metadata, and unilaterally stripping it before producing documents can draw scrutiny even without bad intent. Williams v. Sprint/United Management Co. established that documents ordered produced in their ordinary business format should generally include metadata unless the parties agree otherwise or the court rules on an objection first.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Can metadata alone get a case dismissed?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Metadata is rarely the only factor, but it is frequently the thread that unravels everything else. In Gilimex v. Amazon, the file-creation metadata combined with inconsistencies in physical document seals and an implausible cover story to convince the court that the fabrication went beyond a single bad document, which is what justified dismissing the entire case rather than excluding individual exhibits.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Was the Iraq Dossier case an actual lawsuit?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">No. The 2003 UK government dossier on Iraq was never the subject of a lawsuit. It's included here because it remains the most widely known example of document metadata changing a real-world outcome, in this case a major political and journalistic one rather than a courtroom verdict.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          How do forensic examiners detect a print-to-PDF fabrication?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Examiners typically compare the claimed origin of a document against what its metadata actually shows. A document claimed to be a scan of a years-old paper original should generally not carry a Producer or CreatorTool field pointing to spreadsheet export software, and its creation date should not cluster suspiciously close to the discovery deadline. Examiners also compare metadata across a batch of supposedly unrelated documents, since genuine scans rarely share identical creation timestamps or software fingerprints.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Limitations</h3>
    <p>This article summarizes publicly reported facts from court opinions, contemporaneous news coverage, and parliamentary records; it is not a substitute for reading the underlying filings, and readers relying on these cases for their own legal work should consult the primary sources directly. A quick metadata check using a tool like the one described here can tell you whether something is worth escalating, but none of the outcomes described in this article rested on a casual property-panel glance alone. Each required a qualified forensic examiner, sworn testimony, and in most cases extensive cross-examination of the parties' own explanations. Metadata can also be misleading on its own: routine actions like downloading or re-saving a file can update timestamps without any content change, which is exactly why courts weigh metadata alongside other evidence rather than treating a single field as conclusive.</p>

